Z||000016||Tryxalon||08-03-2001||09:39 AM||||The voice of Experience:  No "coil" is neccesary.  Just a direct wire setup.  <P>You want to position the sparkplug at least 12 inches behind the muffler (to prevent detonating the muffler) and it needs at least 6 inches after the plug (to form a controlled cone that will clear your bodywork).  A momentary "on"  switch on the dash to "flame on" is all that is needed. The spark plug tip needs to be as close to "center" of the exhaust pipe as possible, and you'd have to experiment with the gap ...   point 05 was the standard, expand to get stronger flame, tighten the gap to get quicker burn.  <P>Now the Bad Stuff: <P>#1 Law enforcement does not take kindly to such a setup (I "torched" the front end of a Texas County Mounty cruiser on a back road in 69. Seems it caught the paint on fire. He was not thrilled ... but he shouldn't have been tailgating!! Fast talking, dark country road so he couldn't find the modification, and a lie about how it backfired a lot saved me from arrest and a lawsuit.  Still had fines for "operating a motor vehicle in a dangerous condition") <P>#2 You get about 5 to 7 "flames" before it eats through a standard exhaust pipe.  <P>#3 IF there is too much fuel in the exhaust .. the combustion can travel back up into the Muffler (Ka-boom!) or even further back.   For this reason, and the enclosed engine space -- I don't think I would do such anymore. <P>  Maybe on a pickup (front engine, rear wheel drive and the Gas tank behind the Cab) with the Exhaust outlet 4 to six feet behind the Gas tank and 4 feet of exhaust pipe and a muffler between the engine and the "kaboom".  But not a current car and definately not a mid engined car where the exhaust wraps around the engine and sits less than 18 inches away from the fuel lines and the Gas tank!! <P> Not in a FIero ... or it might really be "FLAME ON"!!! (cause plastic burns SO WELL!!) <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 08-03-2001).]||||reg||1
